  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY NIGHT THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 208 AM CDT Mon Oct 5 2026\n\nTuesday night into Wednesday, the upper-level ridge across the \nwestern United States will begin to flatten as a northern-stream \nlongwave trough moves southeastward into southern Canada. Warm air \nadvection will persist Tuesday night into Wednesday ahead of this \nsystem, with 850-mb temperatures forecast to remain around 10 to 12 \ndegrees above average. The combination of the anomalously warm air \nmass and increasing low-level moisture should keep overnight \ntemperatures mild, with lows generally in the lower to middle 50s \nTuesday night. Through the day Wednesday, the longwave trough will \ncontinue moving southeastward, resulting in strengthening \nnorthwesterly flow aloft across the northern and central Plains. \nMeanwhile, surface low pressure will deepen along the lee of the \nRockies ahead of an approaching shortwave disturbance. Increasing \nsoutherly to southeasterly low-level flow ahead of the surface low \nshould allow moisture to return northward, with surface dew points \npotentially reaching the lower to middle 60s across portions of the \narea. Highs Wednesday should reach the middle 70s across most of the \narea, with lower 80s possible across southwest Nebraska. Wednesday \nevening into Wednesday night, a frontal boundary associated with the \nnorthern-stream trough will move southeastward into the area. \nIncreasing low-level moisture combined with forcing along the \nboundary should support at least some potential for showers and \nthunderstorms. Considerable uncertainty remains regarding the \ncoverage and location of precipitation, though the better potential \ncurrently appears to favor central and eastern portions of the \nforecast area. Trends will continue to be monitored over the next \nseveral forecast cycles.\n\nThursday, surface high pressure will move across the region as the \nnorthern-stream trough progresses eastward across the eastern CONUS. \nSoutheasterly low-level flow should maintain moisture across the \nregion. Additional precipitation remains possible, primarily across \neastern portions of the forecast area. Highs Thursday should remain \nin the lower to middle 70s, with overnight lows generally in the \nlower 50s.\n\nFriday into early next week, confidence is increasing in a \ntransition toward a more amplified and potentially active weather \npattern. An upper-level trough is forecast to move into the Pacific \nNorthwest Friday before progressing into the western United States \nover the weekend. As this occurs, the western ridge should shift \neastward, allowing southwesterly flow aloft to become established \nacross the central Plains. There is also increasing support from the \nbroader hemispheric pattern for this transition, with teleconnection \nforecasts trending toward negative PNA, EPO, and AO regimes by early \nnext week. The MJO is also forecast to progress toward Phase 1. \nWhile these signals lend additional support to a transition away \nfrom the persistent western-ridge pattern, considerable uncertainty \nremains regarding the magnitude and evolution of the western trough. \nNotable differences remain among model guidance, with the GFS/GEFS \nfavoring a deeper northern-stream trough across the Hudson Bay \nregion, which would support a stronger southward push of colder air \ninto the region. In contrast, the ECMWF maintains ridging across the \nHudson Bay region, keeping the colder air displaced farther north. \nEven with these disparities, confidence is gradually increasing in a \nmore active pattern by late weekend into early next week, with \ngreater precipitation chances and the potential for a transition \ntoward below-normal temperatures. This pattern evolution will \ncontinue to be monitored over the next few days.",
